From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Ask for the faster mode first and the larger one after

The frames leaving the encoder were found to be exactly evenly spaced, to the
tenth of a millisecond, while what arrived at the receiver still moved badly.
Every frame sharp, every frame on time, and the movement between them wrong.

Which leaves what the even spacing is sampling. Thirty frames a second taken
at perfectly regular moments land at irregular ones in material that was not
made at thirty: some of its frames are taken twice and some once, and that is
seen as movement that stutters however good each picture is. The same
material played on the machine itself looks right, because nothing is
resampling it there.

Sixty divides evenly into material made at sixty, and falls closer on
twenty-four and twenty-five than thirty does. So the faster mode is asked for
first, and the larger one is kept behind it for receivers that do not offer
it. Which is wanted is a matter of what is being watched, and both are now
written where that can be said.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
@@ -258,6 +258,7 @@
   gint height;
   gint refresh_rate;
 } wfd_preferred_modes[] = {
+  { 1280, 720, 60 },
   { 1920, 1080, 30 },
 };
 
